<h1>The Global AI Literacy Policy Landscape: An Accelerating Wave of Institutionalization</h1>

<p>On February 2, 2025, Article 4 of the EU AI Act came into effect. This provision does not target high-risk AI systems, nor does it impose transparency obligations on generative AI. Instead, it establishes a foundational requirement for everyone:</p>

<blockquote>Providers and deployers shall take measures to ensure, to the best extent possible, a sufficient level of <strong>AI literacy</strong> of their staff and other persons dealing with the operation and use of AI systems on their behalf.</blockquote>

<p>This marks the first time in any major economy that AI literacy has been codified as a legal obligation. Non-compliance may lead to penalties.</p>

<p>In the same year, the U.S. President signed an executive order establishing a White House Task Force on AI Education. Singapore's Prime Minister declared AI literacy a national priority in his Budget 2026 speech and formed a National AI Council chaired by himself. Japan's cabinet approved its first AI Basic Plan, committing over ¥1 trillion (approximately $6.7 billion USD) to AI development and literacy. China's Ministry of Education, together with four other ministries, released the "AI + Education" Action Plan, aiming to build a comprehensive AI education system spanning K-12 to higher education and lifelong learning.</p>

<p>Across 2025 and 2026, the world's major economies nearly simultaneously institutionalized AI literacy. This is not a coincidence—it is a coordinated policy response to the moment AI reached a critical penetration threshold in society.</p>

<h2>I. Defining AI Literacy</h2>

<p>Before examining policies, it is essential to clarify what AI literacy means.</p>

<p>AI literacy is <strong>not</strong> the ability to use ChatGPT or other tools. The UNESCO AI Competency Framework for Students, released in September 2024, defines AI literacy across four dimensions:</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>Human-centred mindset</strong>: Understanding one's relationship with AI and maintaining agency</li>
<li><strong>Ethics of AI</strong>: Recognizing bias, privacy risks, and ethical dilemmas</li>
<li><strong>AI techniques and applications</strong>: Understanding basic principles and using tools effectively</li>
<li><strong>AI system design</strong>: Participating in AI system design from a user perspective</li>
</ol>

<p>These four dimensions span three progression levels: Understand → Apply → Create, comprising 12 specific competency modules.</p>

<p>The framework's breakthrough is that it is <strong>not a technical training manual</strong>, but a <strong>citizenship framework</strong>. It positions AI literacy alongside information literacy, media literacy, and digital literacy—not for training AI engineers, but for preparing responsible citizens in the AI age.</p>

<p>The companion framework for teachers, released simultaneously, adds "AI pedagogy" and "AI for professional learning" as additional dimensions, totaling 15 competencies.</p>

<p>UNESCO's framework provides a global reference, but nations have charted distinctly different paths based on their institutional traditions and policy priorities.</p>

<h2>II. Policy Landscapes: Five Distinct Pathways</h2>

<h3>2.1 The European Union: Regulation-Driven</h3>

<p>The EU is the only major economy to embed AI literacy in hard law. Article 4 of the AI Act applies to all AI systems regardless of risk level, and to all providers and deployers operating within the EU. Any company with EU operations must now demonstrate that its employees possess "sufficient AI literacy."</p>

<p>The early activation of Article 4—taking effect in February 2025, well before most other provisions of the Act—conveys a clear signal: AI governance begins with people, not technology.</p>

<p>In May 2025, the European AI Office published a Q&A clarifying that: (1) the obligation covers all staff, not just technical roles; (2) the required level of literacy depends on the specific context of AI use; and (3) organizations should design training based on employees' education, experience, and use scenarios.</p>

<p>Starting August 2026, national market surveillance authorities in each member state can enforce the literacy obligation and impose penalties for non-compliance.</p>

<p>The AI Continent Action Plan, released in April 2025, further positions AI literacy as one of four strategic pillars, aiming to expand skills training through AI Skills Academies and European Digital Innovation Hubs.</p>

<p><strong>Pathway characteristic</strong>: Regulation-first, using compliance pressure to drive organizational and individual capability building.</p>

<h3>2.2 The United States: Federal Guidance, State Innovation</h3>

<p>The U.S. approach is defined by its decentralized structure. The federal executive order provides a guiding framework, while specific educational standards are left to individual states.</p>

<p>On April 23, 2025, President Trump signed the "Advancing Artificial Intelligence Education for American Youth" executive order. Key measures include: a White House Task Force on AI Education, development of K-12 AI resources, a teacher training program, and a nationwide "Presidential AI Challenge."</p>

<p>As of June 2026, 37 states and Puerto Rico had published official K-12 AI guidance. This number stood at 34 in October 2025, reflecting accelerating state-level momentum. Ohio became the first state to mandate that every K-12 school district adopt a formal AI use policy (by July 1, 2026).</p>

<p>March 27, 2026 was designated as National AI Literacy Day, a multi-organizational initiative targeting K-16 students, educators, and communities.</p>

<p><strong>Pathway characteristic</strong>: Federal framework guidance, decentralized state implementation, strong civil society participation, accelerating momentum.</p>

<h3>2.3 Singapore: National Strategy-Driven</h3>

<p>Singapore embeds its AI literacy policy within the national AI strategy (NAIS 2.0) and the EdTech Masterplan 2030. As a city-state, Singapore possesses strong centralized coordination capabilities.</p>

<p>In his Budget 2026 speech, Prime Minister Lawrence Wong elevated AI literacy to a national priority, announcing the establishment of a National AI Council chaired by himself, strengthened AI literacy across all institutes of higher learning, redesigned SkillsFuture learning pathways, and expanded tax incentives for AI-related business expenditure.</p>

<p>On the education front, Singapore's Ministry of Education introduced age-tiered AI usage policies in 2026 with calibrated intervention:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Primary 1-3</strong>: No direct AI use; print-first, tactile learning emphasis</li>
<li><strong>Primary 4-6</strong>: Structured, limited AI use under classroom supervision with MOE-developed tools</li>
<li><strong>Secondary and above</strong>: Graduated AI exposure through the Student Learning Space platform</li>
</ul>

<p>MOE also proposed four AI literacy pillars: Learn about AI, Learn to use AI, Learn with AI, and Learn beyond AI.</p>

<p>In February 2026, the Institute of Policy Studies at NUS published the "Unified AI Literacy Framework for Singapore," providing a common reference for cross-sector policy design.</p>

<p><strong>Pathway characteristic</strong>: Strong central coordination, national strategy-driven, deep coupling between education and industrial policy, fine-grained age-tiered design.</p>

<h3>2.4 Japan: Progressive Upgrade from IT to AI Literacy</h3>

<p>Japan has taken a progressive, upgrading approach. As early as 2018, Japan introduced programming education in primary and secondary schools. Since 2022, "Jōhō I" (Information I) has been a compulsory high school subject.</p>

<p>In December 2024, the Ministry of Education (MEXT) released Version 2.0 of its guidelines on generative AI use in schools, establishing a basic framework and five core principles for integrating AI in education.</p>

<p>The turning point came in December 2025 when the cabinet approved Japan's first AI Basic Plan. The plan explicitly lists "universal AI literacy education" and "workforce reskilling" as two of four core policy pillars. With a five-year budget of ¥1 trillion (approx. $6.7B), the goal is to raise national AI usage rates to 80%.</p>

<p>In higher education, the MDASH (Mathematics, Data Science, and AI Education Program Certification) system covers 590 universities, training approximately 500,000 students annually across two tiers: Literacy Level and Applied Basic Level.</p>

<p><strong>Pathway characteristic</strong>: Progressive upgrade from programming to AI literacy; synchronized education and industrial investment; broad higher education coverage.</p>

<h3>2.5 China: Top-Down Planning with Local Pilots</h3>

<p>China's AI literacy policy follows a classic "central direction, local implementation" model. Unlike the EU's compliance-driven approach or Singapore's strategic centralization, China leverages administrative power with scalable pilot programs.</p>

<p>In November 2024, the Ministry of Education issued a notice aiming to achieve universal AI education in primary and secondary schools by 2030.</p>

<p>In May 2025, the MOE's Basic Education Teaching Advisory Committee published the "AI General Education Guide (2025)" and the "Generative AI Usage Guide for Students (2025)."</p>

<p>In August 2025, the State Council released opinions on deepening the "AI+" initiative (State Council Document No. 11 of 2025), positioning AI as a core enabling technology across all economic sectors.</p>

<p>In April 2026, the Ministry of Education, together with the National Development and Reform Commission, the Ministry of Industry and Information Technology, the Ministry of Science and Technology, and the National Data Administration, jointly issued the "AI + Education" Action Plan—the highest-level AI literacy policy document to date. The plan aims to build "vertically coherent, horizontally connected AI education spanning all school levels and a universal general education system."</p>

<p>At the local level, Beijing has been the most aggressive—implementing a minimum of 8 AI education hours per school year per student from fall 2025, covering all grades through high school, with a "100 Master + 1000 Seed Teachers" training program. Shandong has a 10-department joint implementation plan. Smaller cities like Tongling have also issued detailed curricula.</p>

<p><strong>Pathway characteristic</strong>: Central planning with local piloting; strong administrative push; ambitious schedules with local variation. 2026-2030 is the critical execution window.</p>

<h3>2.6 UNESCO: The Global Reference</h3>

<p>UNESCO is the only international organization that has developed global AI literacy standards. Its two frameworks, released in September 2024, have already influenced curriculum design in multiple countries. The Student Framework (4 dimensions, 12 competencies) and Teacher Framework (5 dimensions, 15 competencies) serve as common reference points.</p>

<p>Beyond the frameworks, UNESCO has also published "AI and Education: Guidance for Policy-makers" (2021) and "Guidance for Generative AI in Education and Research" (2023), providing systematic decision-making support for policy designers.</p>

<h2>III. Comparative Analysis</h2>

<h3>3.1 Common Patterns</h3>

<ol>
<li><strong>Temporal concentration</strong>: 2025-2026 marks a concentrated enactment period globally. This aligns with the typical two-year policy response lag following ChatGPT's breakthrough in late 2022.</li>
<li><strong>K-12 as primary entry point</strong>: Most policies target school education as the starting point for AI literacy.</li>
<li><strong>Lifelong learning perspective</strong>: Nearly all systems cover teacher training and adult reskilling.</li>
<li><strong>UNESCO framework as reference base</strong>: The EU, Singapore, and Japan all reference the UNESCO framework when designing their own standards.</li>
</ol>

<h3>3.2 Divergent Pathways</h3>

<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Dimension</th><th>EU</th><th>US</th><th>Singapore</th><th>Japan</th><th>China</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Driver</td><td>Hard law</td><td>Executive order + state autonomy</td><td>National strategy</td><td>Industry policy + education</td><td>Central planning + pilots</td></tr>
<tr><td>Priority</td><td>Corporate compliance</td><td>K-12 education</td><td>Full chain</td><td>Higher ed + talent pipeline</td><td>K-12 → higher ed → universal</td></tr>
<tr><td>Timeline</td><td>2025 (effective)</td><td>State-by-state</td><td>Already implementing</td><td>2026-2031 window</td><td>2030 target</td></tr>
<tr><td>Distinctive feature</td><td>AI literacy Q&A + penalties</td><td>White House Task Force + NAIL Day</td><td>National AI Council + 4 pillars</td><td>¥1T investment</td><td>5-ministerial plan + local pilots</td></tr>
<tr><td>Coverage logic</td><td>Organizational obligation</td><td>School autonomy</td><td>Government-led</td><td>Public-private unified</td><td>Administrative-driven</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>3.3 Unresolved Challenges</h3>

<p>Despite significant momentum, several challenges remain:</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>Lack of standardized assessment</strong>: How do you measure "sufficient AI literacy"? UNESCO provides content dimensions but no quantifiable assessment tools.</li>
<li><strong>Teacher shortage</strong>: Globally, AI literacy education faces severe talent shortages. OECD TALIS 2024 data shows only 16-20% of Japanese teachers use AI in classrooms.</li>
<li><strong>Digital divide risk</strong>: Affluent regions and schools can advance AI education faster, potentially widening educational gaps.</li>
<li><strong>Conflation of literacy with tool training</strong>: Many implementations reduce AI literacy to "how to use ChatGPT," deviating from the UNESCO framework's broader vision encompassing ethics, bias, and human-centred thinking.</li>
</ol>

<h2>IV. Implications for Individuals</h2>

<p>Policies are advancing, but implementation takes time.</p>

<p>For individuals, the key takeaway is that the <strong>institutional contract</strong> for AI literacy has been signed—governments and international organizations have made clear that AI literacy is not optional. But the actual assessment tools, curricula, and certification systems remain immature. A <strong>policy-implementation gap</strong> exists.</p>

<p>This gap is where AI literacy diagnostic tools come into play: helping individuals map their own AI literacy before the educational infrastructure fully catches up.</p>

